      "hashtags": [],
      "source_text": "Seven programs, same goal, completely different timelines I've been staring at this chart for a while now. Seven eVTOL programs. Same mission: build a certified aircraft people can actually buy. But look at how differently they're moving. Some companies locked in their design in 2015. Others were still changing it in 2023. Some went from program start to full-scale piloted testing in five years. Others took longer—or redesigned partway through. Some programs are still going. Some shut down. So, what's driving this? I don't think it's talent. These teams have some of the best engineers in the world. It's not just money, either. Many of these companies raised nine figures. There's something else. A decision made early—sometimes before the first prototype, sometimes before the full team is even hired. It looks purely technical at the time. But it creates a gap: Suppliers you don't have. Testing rigs you haven't built. Certification paths you've never walked. Processes that must scale faster than your team can learn. Some programs saw this gap coming and adjusted. Others discovered it years later, after burning through tens of millions of dollars. After 34 years in aerospace—including eVTOLs that flew, I've learned this: The most expensive mistakes aren't technical failures. They're mismatches between what you're trying to build and what your organization can actually handle. So, here's my question: If you're investing $50M in an eVTOL program, what do you ask before you write the check—beyond \"Can this thing fly?\" I’ve been tracking seven leading eVTOL programs for years — what pattern do you see when you look at their development timelines?",
